#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Flash a Hue light when something loud is about to pass over the house.

The design constraints below are not preferences. Each came out of the original
discussion recorded in docs/design/pi-panel/ADSB-HANDOFF.md §6, and every one of
them describes a way this feature could do harm if built carelessly.

1. IT MUST NOT LOOK LIKE A MEDICAL ALARM
   The bedside panel reserves klaxon red for high-priority physiological alarm
   and it "appears nowhere else". A light that turns a bedroom red at 3am, for
   an aeroplane, teaches the household to distrust the colour that matters. The
   aircraft channel is the radar's cyan, and amber only for the loudest band.

2. IT MUST RESTORE WHAT IT FOUND
   This is the one that can actually hurt: leaving a bedroom lamp on at 3am, or
   leaving it a colour it was never set to. The previous state of every light
   touched is captured BEFORE the first change and restored afterwards --
   including on failure, and including on shutdown. A light that was off goes
   back off; a light that was warm white at 40% goes back to warm white at 40%.

3. IT MUST NOT FIRE PER AIRCRAFT
   Portland traffic is continuous. Without a cooldown this would strobe a
   bedroom all evening and be unplugged within a day, which is the same as not
   existing. One warning, then quiet for the cooldown.

4. IT MUST FAIL LOUDLY
   A silent failure here is indistinguishable from a quiet sky, which is exactly
   the state this system refuses to conflate. Bridge unreachable, auth rejected,
   light missing: all logged with the blocker named, and surfaced in the status
   file the panel reads.

5. IT MUST NOT BE THE ALARM
   This is an early warning that a noise is coming, so it can be braced for. It
   is not armed, it controls nothing, and nothing depends on it.

Why the local v1 API
--------------------
`/api/<username>/lights/<id>/state` needs no cloud account and no internet: the
bridge is on the LAN and the Pi talks to it directly. v2 exists and v1 is
formally deprecated, but v1 is still supported on current bridges (this one
reports apiversion 1.78.0) and is dramatically simpler -- v2 requires an
event-stream client and TLS with a bridge-specific certificate for what is, for
us, three field writes.
"""

